---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const coverImage = "/images/podcasts/phds-on-the-parsha-cover.png";
const trailerAudio = "/audio/phds-on-the-parsha/trailer.wav";

const launchParsha = {
  name: "Bereishit",
  ref: "Genesis 1:1–6:8",
  reason: "The launch episode ties directly to Simchat Torah, when the annual Torah-reading cycle restarts from the very first verse — the natural moment to premiere a show about the scholarship hiding inside every parsha.",
};

const topics = [
  { title: "The Two Creation Accounts", verses: "Genesis 1:1–2:3 & 2:4–25", note: "Source criticism, literary structure, and the documentary hypothesis" },
  { title: "Image of God (Imago Dei)", verses: "Genesis 1:26–27", note: "What \"in our image, after our likeness\" meant in the ancient Near East" },
  { title: "Eden & the Fall", verses: "Genesis 2:8–3:24", note: "Theodicy, gender, and the history of interpretation" },
  { title: "Cain and Abel", verses: "Genesis 4:1–16", note: "The first murder, the mark of Cain, and sibling rivalry as literary type-scene" },
  { title: "Genealogies of Genesis 5", verses: "Genesis 5:1–32", note: "Numerology, longevity, and ANE king-list parallels" },
  { title: "Sons of God / Nephilim", verses: "Genesis 6:1–4", note: "One of the strangest four verses in the Torah — and a magnet for dissertations" },
];

const emailTemplate = `Subject: Invitation to join me on "PhDs on the Parsha" — {{DISSERTATION_TOPIC}}

Hi Dr. {{LAST_NAME}},

My name is Matthew Miller — I host "New Books in Jewish Studies" on the New Books Network, and I'm launching a new companion show called "PhDs on the Parsha." Each episode pairs the week's Torah portion with a conversation with the scholar who wrote their doctoral work on a theme, character, or verse range inside it.

I came across your dissertation, "{{DISSERTATION_TITLE}}" ({{INSTITUTION}}, {{YEAR}}), and it's a perfect fit for our launch episode on Parashat Bereishit (Genesis 1:1–6:8). I'd love to have you on for a 30–45 minute conversation — accessible to a lay audience, but built on your actual research.

Logistics are easy: remote recording (video or audio-only, your choice), roughly 30-45 minutes, and I handle all editing and production. No prep required beyond being ready to talk about your own work.

Would you be open to it? Happy to work around your schedule — episodes for this parsha would ideally record in the next few weeks, but I'm flexible.

Thanks for considering it, and for the work itself — it's exactly the kind of scholarship this show exists to surface.

Best,
Matthew Miller
The Other Matthew Miller / New Books in Jewish Studies
https://theothermatthewmiller.com`;
---

<Layout
  title="PhDs on the Parsha"
  description="A new podcast from Matthew Miller: each week, the scholar who wrote the dissertation on that week's Torah portion. Launching with Parashat Bereishit."
  image={coverImage}
>
  <main class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16 max-w-4xl">
    <header class="text-center space-y-6 mb-14">
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark">
        A New Show From The Other Matthew Miller
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">
        PhDs on the Parsha
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Every week, Jews around the world read the same parsha. Somewhere, someone spent
        seven years — and staked their PhD — on just one of its verses. This is the show
        where the two meet.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg max-w-md mx-auto border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark">
        <img
          src={coverImage}
          alt="PhDs on the Parsha podcast cover art: a Torah scroll transitioning into a stack of academic books topped with a graduation cap"
          class="w-full h-auto"
          loading="eager"
        />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-14 bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">Trailer</h2>
      <p class="text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark">
        A ~60-second teaser for the launch. Voiceover generated for the pilot cut — swap in
        your own read whenever you're ready.
      </p>
      <audio controls class="w-full" preload="none">
        <source src={trailerAudio} type="audio/wav" />
        Your browser does not support the audio element.
      </audio>
      <details class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium">Trailer script</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed">
In the beginning, there was a question.

Six days. One verse can launch a thousand-page dissertation.

Every week, Jews around the world open the same page, read the same words — but somewhere,
someone spent seven years, and staked their PhD, on just one of those verses.

I'm Matthew Miller. And this is PhDs on the Parsha: the show where the weekly Torah portion
meets the doctoral defense.

Every episode, I sit down with the scholar who wrote the dissertation on your parsha —
Creation, Eden, Cain and Abel, the sons of God — and we go deeper than the margin notes ever could.

Episode one: Bereishit. In the beginning.

PhDs on the Parsha. Wherever you get your podcasts.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">The concept</h2>
      <p class="text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
        Every parsha touches dozens of live academic fault lines — source criticism,
        ancient Near Eastern parallels, gender and literary criticism, reception history.
        Someone, somewhere, wrote 200+ pages defending an argument about a single verse
        you'll hear read aloud in shul this week. <strong>PhDs on the Parsha</strong> finds
        that person and has the conversation the footnotes never let them have out loud.
      </p>
      <p class="text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
        Format: ~30–45 minute conversations, one guest per episode, timed to release
        alongside (or just before) the parsha it covers. Produced under the same banner as
        <a href="/jewish-studies" class="text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark hover:underline">New Books in Jewish Studies</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">
        Launch episode: Parashat {launchParsha.name} <span class="text-base font-normal text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark">({launchParsha.ref})</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">{launchParsha.reason}</p>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-4">
        {topics.map(topic => (
          <div class="bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-lg p-4">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">{topic.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark mt-1">{topic.verses}</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark mt-2">{topic.note}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">Guest outreach</h2>
      <p class="text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
        A researched shortlist of scholars whose dissertations map onto the Bereishit topics
        above — with contact details where publicly available — is compiled separately as a
        CSV for outreach tracking, alongside a draft invitation email.
      </p>
      <details class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-lg p-4">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">Draft outreach email template</summary>
        <pre class="mt-3 whitespace-pre-wrap font-sans leading-relaxed">{emailTemplate}</pre>
      </details>
    </section>

    <footer class="pt-8 border-t border-border-light dark:border-border-dark text-center text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark">
      <p>Part of <a href="/" class="text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark hover:underline">The Other Matthew Miller</a> — alongside <a href="/jewish-studies" class="text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark hover:underline">New Books in Jewish Studies</a>.</p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
